  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+3 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/6bc5aed39e6421b8ff2416edb75dc888c8f38ae14f99b3d6e28d8e4c9302908f.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$",
